## 9. Potential Improvements

### Architecture Weaknesses
- **State Management**: The API relies on `IORef` memory state (`weightsRef`) mapping. It is memory volatile, meaning every restart causes it to retrain. The model state should ideally be serialized to disk for immediate application mapping.
- **Error Handling**: [app.js](file:///e:/antigravity%20haskell%20-%20Copy/frontend/app.js) catches API network errors effectively, but [Main.hs](file:///e:/antigravity%20haskell%20-%20Copy/backend/heatwave-backend/app/Main.hs) could map deeper HTTP standard behaviors (ex: Mapping HTTP `400 Bad Request` explicitly if JSON structural parameters missing).

### Machine Learning
- **Dataset Scaling**: Uses naive Full-Batch memory Gradient Descent mapping via standard lists. Changing it to Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) or Mini-Batch logic would prevent scaling bottlenecks if [data.csv](file:///e:/antigravity%20haskell%20-%20Copy/backend/heatwave-backend/data.csv) becomes exponentially larger.
- **Normalization**: Features like temperature vs humidity run inherently differently mathematically. The codebase is missing an explicit Z-Score Standardization map inside [ML.hs](file:///e:/antigravity%20haskell%20-%20Copy/backend/heatwave-backend/src/ML.hs) avoiding gradient convergence lag.

### Scalability and Deployment
- The system uses SQLite locally. Shifting application state mappings off SQLite memory maps into formal Postgres pools increases write locks functionality across scaled pods.

---

## 10. Developer Summary

The application operates as a standalone predictive modeling Full-Stack Web App. 

As a **frontend developer**, your logic loops through [app.js](file:///e:/antigravity%20haskell%20-%20Copy/frontend/app.js) focusing on updating DOM representations through `Fetch` payloads connecting JSON payloads locally inside the memory states of the component boundaries.

As a **backend / data developer**, your application relies on Haskell's type-safety mechanics. If you want to modify a database route, mutate the schema inside `initDB (Database.hs)`, update JSON structural models [(Types.hs)](file:///e:/antigravity%20haskell%20-%20Copy/frontend/app.js#3-14), and parse it strictly using HTTP middleware mapping [(Main.hs)](file:///e:/antigravity%20haskell%20-%20Copy/frontend/app.js#3-14). Modifying the actual intelligence occurs completely decoupled securely within pure functional boundaries [(ML.hs)](file:///e:/antigravity%20haskell%20-%20Copy/frontend/app.js#3-14).

It is clean, robust, structurally separated, perfectly type-checked, and relies on 0 machine learning abstraction libraries.
